\title{tabtools: publication-ready descriptive and regression tables}
\description{
Baseline-characteristics tables, regression tables, incidence-rate tables,
treatment-effect tables and weight diagnostics, written to Excel (with a
house style), CSV and Markdown, or converted to flextable, gt, gtsummary
and tinytable. tabtools is an R implementation of the Stata \code{tabtools}
commands (Stata package version 2.1.14): everything a reader sees -- row
labels, category indentation, reference rows, number formats, headers,
and Excel styling -- matches the Stata output cell for cell, while
hypothesis tests use R's conventional defaults.
}
\details{
Start with \code{\link[=table1_tc]{table1_tc()}} (a "Table 1" of baseline characteristics) and
\code{\link[=regtab]{regtab()}} (fitted models side by side); \code{vignette("tabtools")} walks
through both. By task:
\itemize{
\item Describe a sample: \code{\link[=table1_tc]{table1_tc()}} (alias \code{\link[=desctab]{desctab()}}).
\item Show regression models: \code{\link[=regtab]{regtab()}}, with \code{\link[=regtab_uv]{regtab_uv()}} for univariable
models, \code{\link[=tt_mi]{tt_mi()}} for multiply imputed fits, and \code{\link[=tt_vcov]{tt_vcov()}} for the
variance behind the standard errors; \code{vignette("regression-tables")}.
\item Incidence rates and the cohort-study "Table 2": \code{\link[=tt_rates]{tt_rates()}} computes
the rates, \code{\link[=stratetab]{stratetab()}} tabulates them, and \code{\link[=hrcomptab]{hrcomptab()}} puts hazard
ratios from \code{\link[=regtab]{regtab()}} beside them.
\item Treatment effects and margins (from marginaleffects): \code{\link[=effecttab]{effecttab()}}.
\item Inverse probability weights: \code{\link[=wttab]{wttab()}}; \code{vignette("weighted-analyses")}.
\item Combine tables: \code{\link[=tt_merge]{tt_merge()}} (side by side), \code{\link[=tt_stack]{tt_stack()}} (one under
another), \code{\link[=comptab]{comptab()}} (selected rows of model tables); \code{\link[=puttab]{puttab()}}
styles any data frame or matrix, and \code{\link[=stacktab]{stacktab()}} assembles Excel
sheets into one composite.
\item Write or convert: the commands' \code{xlsx}, \code{csv} and \code{markdown}
arguments, or \code{\link[=tt_write_xlsx]{tt_write_xlsx()}}, \code{\link[=tt_write_csv]{tt_write_csv()}},
\code{\link[=tt_write_markdown]{tt_write_markdown()}}, \code{\link[=as_flextable.tt_table]{as_flextable.tt_table()}}, \code{\link[=tt_as_gt]{tt_as_gt()}},
\code{\link[=tt_as_gtsummary]{tt_as_gtsummary()}}, \code{\link[=tt_as_tinytable]{tt_as_tinytable()}}; house-style defaults with
\code{\link[=tabtools_options]{tabtools_options()}}.
In every command \code{sheet}, \code{open} and \code{mdappend} need their target:
\code{sheet} or \code{open} without \code{xlsx}, or \code{mdappend} without \code{markdown}, is
an error (\code{sheet = NULL} is the same as leaving it out). Stata's
\code{desctab} refuses \code{sheet()} without \code{excel()} too; its other commands
